Stephen Spackman, Mr. Overlay Man, writes about the Turbo C overlay problem:
-=> Basically what happened is this: since the executable is in the 800K
-=> range we KNEW we needed overlays. Microsoft has an overlay linker;
-=> Borland has not. So we worked with the Microsoft tools.
-=> 
-=> When we finished we tested with the Microsoft linker and Turbo C (an
-=> unusual configuration, but there are a few people who MIGHT have it).
-=> It didn't work and the diagnostics were Strange and Weird. So,
-=> figuring it wasn't very important anyway (most people with Turbo
-=> presumably use tlink, which doesn't support overlays), we turned out
-=> attention to performance, which is now our main concern.

Unusual, but required.  As i wrote in Makefile.tcc, TLINK has never
been able to handle NetHack's 90 Huge-model objects, and will gladly
produce unoverlayed overlarge executables that won't fit in 640K.  For
Turbo C, you have no choice but to use the Microsoft Linker, OVERLAY
or no.

Rumors on BIX suggest that Borland's internal linkers now do overlay (with
a myriad of tiny overlays, something they call VROOM), and this may be in
next release of Turbo C.  I look forward to it.

The Mystic Errors are Fixup overflows on ovlmgr.obj.  Of late, i get
Fixup overflows on various data segments even without OVERLAY defined.
Perhaps Turbo C puts all its eggs in one data segment which exceeds
64K, where MSC uses separate ones (i haven't tried frobbing tcc's -z
set segment names parameter).  Perhaps my antiquated LINK 3.06 tries
to combine too many data segments (i have no later version to try).
Perhaps MSC optimizes data in a way Turbo C doesn't (even with everything
enabled compared to nothing?  i doubt).

The tireless Turbo C developer is tired.  I'll gladly e-mail my map
files with error lists to anyone who thinks they can grok the prob.
Meanwhile, i'd druther let you compile me a NetHack with everything
(and extra pepperoni), than further strip down the Turbo C version.

-=> Please note that the overlay manager included does NOT interact with C
-=> in any way (well, ok, it assumes that the programme uses the
-=> terminate-with-return-code exit method and doesn't longjmp between
-=> overlays, but that should be about the limit of it), it ONLY has hooks
-=> into the linker; and it really is a very well-behaved (if ugly) bit of
-=> code, all things considered. Any Strange Interactions going on are
-=> between C and the linker, not between C and us, as far as we can see.

In other words, folks, we can't just tailor an ovlmgr.asm for Turbo C.
That isn't the prob.  If LINK 3.06 panics true, some data segment is
oversize with diet Turbo C, but not with chunky MSC.  If LINK panics
false, it may surpass mortal comprehension.

